SUMMARY:Valerie Huhn    A Life Rebuilt in Fingerprints
DESCRIPTION:OPENING RECEPTION: Saturday\, September 19\, 4:00 – 6:00 PM\nEXHIBITION RUNS: September 19 – December 18\, 2026\nMontclair\, NJ — The BrassWorks Gallery is honored and delighted to present a solo exhibition of work by artist Valerie Huhn. The exhibition\, A Life Rebuilt in Fingerprints\, brings together two decades of Huhn’s signature fingerprint-based practice – sculptures\, murals\, and mixed-media works built from thousands of the artist’s own fingerprints – in a body of work that transforms a tool of surveillance and identification into a language of individuality\, memory\, and belonging.\nFor Huhn\, this work is inseparable from her own history. As a teenager\, she survived a car accident that left her with a traumatic brain injury – a neurological disability she still lives with today. In the aftermath\, a series of misdiagnoses led to her placement in state and long-term psychiatric facilities\, an experience that reshaped the course of her life. “I escaped from them to a world of art\,” Huhn says. “I have hidden this injury from friends\, relatives and coworkers. I understand its power to marginalize.”\nThat history is the root of the fingerprint work she has made for the past twenty years. Using her own right index finger\, Huhn presses thousands of individually dated prints in a spectrum of colors onto sheets of clear acetate – deliberately avoiding black ink\, the color of official booking records\, in favor of a palette that reclaims the fingerprint as a mark of celebration rather than control. In much of the work\, she hole-punches the colored prints into small disks and spears them in groups of four onto pins\, a grouping inspired by the four nucleotides of DNA. She then uses these pins to cover books\, furniture\, clothing\, and other everyday objects\, building dense\, glittering surfaces out of what is normally reduced to a tool of identification. The result is work that pushes back against the systems that once sorted her – and the communities she has lived among – into categories of control\, replacing them with a record of individual\, unrepeatable marks.\nValerie Huhn was honored with an Individual Artist Fellowship in Crafts from the New Jersey State Council on the Arts – one of the most competitive\, sought-after distinctions available to a practicing New Jersey artist\, awarded through an anonymous\, peer-reviewed process. Huhn holds a BFA and MFA from the San Francisco Art Institute and studied at Parsons School of Design and the New School for Social Research. Her fingerprint-based work has been exhibited widely across the United States\, including a 2007 exhibition in Washington\, D.C. that launched her large-scale fingerprint murals\, as well as solo and group shows at institutions including the Hunterdon Art Museum and the Morris Museum. She has completed residencies at chashama in New York and Aferro in Newark\, and her work is held in public and private collections. .\nFor additional information contact Valerie Huhn; call or text 212-227-7522\, email valerie.huhn@gmail.com\, visit valeriehuhn.com\,\nor Instagram.com/valerie.huhn_.\nThe BrassWorks Gallery is open to the public Monday through Friday from 7:00 am to 7:00 pm. Housed in a beautifully restored 1930s industrial building that once served as the George Rutledge Etched Brass Works\, the gallery honors Montclair’s rich history while supporting a vibrant contemporary art community.\nBrassWorks is located at 105 Grove Street\, Montclair\, NJ. Originally constructed during the early twentieth century\, 105 Grove Street was\, for many years\, home to the family-owned George Rutledge Etched Brass Works company. The property was converted into a unique professional office building in 2007 and provides space for a community art gallery. Upon learning the site’s heritage as home to a family-owned brass works company\, the partners decided to transform the building while honoring its past by naming it BrassWorks on Grove. The present building combines modern amenities with the faithful restoration of the original 1930 architectural details. \nShare this:\n				Share on Facebook (Opens in new window)\n				Facebook\n			\n				Share on Bluesky (Opens in new window)\n				Bluesky\n			\n				Share on LinkedIn (Opens in new window)\n				LinkedIn\n			\n				Share on Threads (Opens in new window)\n				Threads\n			\n				Share on Nextdoor (Opens in new window)\n				Nextdoor\n			\n				Share on WhatsApp (Opens in new window)\n				WhatsApp\n			\n				Email a link to a friend (Opens in new window)\n				Email
URL:https://montclairlocal.news/event/valerie-huhn-a-life-rebuilt-in-fingerprints/
LOCATION:The BrassWorks Gallery\, 105 Grove Street\, Montclair\, NJ\, 07068\, United States
